1) Classification: mixed dense contraction plus causal scan/reduction. Outputs are output[B,T,16,64] flattened to hidden_out and final_state[B,16,64,256]. Reductions are over state S=256, source time j within a 256-token chunk, and prior chunks; all accesses are dense. Fixed dimensions are H=16, D=64, S=256, G=1, K=256; B and T are runtime variables and N=ceil(T/256).
2) Canonical math: with a_t=exp(A_t), state recurrence is X_t=a_t*X_{t-1}+hidden_t outer B_t and y_t[d]=sum_s C_t[s]*X_t[d,s]+D_h*hidden_t[d]. The reference evaluates equivalent within-chunk terms y_diag(i,d)=sum_{j<=i}(C_i dot B_j)*exp(sum_{k=j+1..i}A_k)*hidden_j[d], chunk-end states, and a chunk-prefix recurrence. Work is independent across batch, head, and head_dim rows except that B/C are shared across heads and the state-size reduction forms each output. FP32 sums and exponentials are required; causal terms may be streamed or reordered without forming L/M.
3) Layouts: hidden is BF16 contiguous [B,T,H,D]; A is BF16 [B,H,T], contiguous in T but logically transposed by the reference; B/C are BF16 [B,T,1,S], contiguous in S and broadcast across H; D is BF16 [H]; initial/final state is BF16 [B,H,D,S], contiguous in S. Dominant reuse is B/C across H and D, hidden across S, and chunk-local A prefixes. Initial state is read once and final/output are written once. A prefixes and small decay vectors fit registers; B/C slices can be staged, while the full per-head state requires distributed register/shared-memory tiling.
4) Regimes: (a) T<=256 with small B: launch latency, limited parallelism, and initial/final-state traffic dominate; direct recurrence is attractive. (b) moderate/large B with T around one to four chunks: dense contraction throughput and state-tile reuse dominate; chunk factorization can exploit abundant independent tiles. (c) long T with small B: serial chunk/time dependency and synchronization dominate, favoring persistent recurrence if occupancy is sufficient. (d) long T with large B: compute throughput dominates, while materialized per-chunk states and quadratic matrices become bandwidth/capacity risks.
5) Knobs: choose time recurrence versus chunk factorization versus associative scan; tile D/S/time; assign programs over B/H/chunk/D; reuse group-shared B/C; choose recomputation versus temporary storage; fuse D residual and padding masks; specialize one-chunk and aligned-T cases; use BF16 dot operands with FP32 accumulation; control exp/cumsum precision and state tile residency.
6) Skeleton: compute FP32 A prefixes or per-step decays; obtain intra-chunk output and chunk-end update; propagate initial/chunk states in causal order; contract C with the entering/current state; add D residual; mask padded tokens and emit BF16 output/final state. Registers hold A/decay scalars and partial reductions, state tiles are distributed across lanes or staged, and synchronization is needed only for reductions or recurrence boundaries.
7) Pruned families: chunk-factorized decomposition targets throughput-rich multi-chunk workloads and uses dense chunk contractions without expanded B/C, but risks quadratic work/storage; time-recurrent state evolution targets short or low-batch workloads and avoids O(K^2) time interactions, but has serial steps and state pressure; hierarchical associative state scan targets very long sequences with enough parallelism, but its large prefix-state traffic is likely the primary limiter.

